    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "That is the order the names are conventionally listed in — the order you meet them walking up a major scale, one mode per degree — and it is nothing more than that. It says nothing about how any of these sound. Chapter 2 puts them in an order that does."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Every mode but two carries a "
        },
        {
          "text": "characteristic note",
          "marks": ["bold"]
        },
        {
          "text": " — the one tone that separates it from its nearest plain relative. The app tints it on the neck, and this pathway tints it the same way in prose: Dorian's "
        },
        {
          "text": "6",
          "marks": [
            "code",
            {
              "type": "color",
              "tone": "amber"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " and Mixolydian's "
        },
        {
          "text": "b7",
          "marks": [
            "code",
            {
              "type": "color",
              "tone": "amber"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " are amber; Phrygian's "
        },
        {
          "text": "b2",
          "marks": [
            "code",
            {
              "type": "color",
              "tone": "rose"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " and Locrian's "
        },
        {
          "text": "b5",
          "marks": [
            "code",
            {
              "type": "color",
              "tone": "rose"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " are rose; Lydian's "
        },
        {
          "text": "#4",
          "marks": [
            "code",
            {
              "type": "color",
              "tone": "violet"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " is violet. Ionian and Aeolian have none — not a gap, but a fact about them: they're the two everything else here is measured against."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "And these are not jazz-only material, whatever you have been told. Ionian, Aeolian, Dorian and Mixolydian turn up constantly in rock."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Play the two you own side by side."
        }
      ]
